# App layout

Examples and the main int-test fixture are a flat folder. `main.ts` is the
route table. Handlers, layouts, and middleware sit beside it as one module
each.

TODO(COO-47): whether this stays a flat folder or becomes module-scoped
folders.

## File names

Deno snake_case. Role in the suffix: `home_route.tsx`, `root_layout.tsx`,
`logger_middleware.ts`. No `routes/` directory and no `_` magic prefixes.

`errors.tsx` holds the `serve()` options `notFound`, `error`, and
`errorFallback` as named exports. Those are not routes.

Ordinary extra modules (`state.ts`, later `db.ts`) sit beside the handlers.
No `layouts/`, `middleware/`, or feature folders.

## Exports

Named exports: `import { Home } from "./home_route.tsx"`. A component that
returns `Element` is PascalCase (`Home`, `RootLayout`, `ErrorPage`).
Middleware and per-method handlers are camelCase (`logger`, `list`, `add`).
One route per file. `/guestbook` is one file with `list` and `add` (two
methods, one route). Do not colocate several routes in `main.ts`.

A route file exports the handler(s) plus local helpers and types it needs.
Path, layouts, and middleware stay in the table in `main.ts`. A file named
`home_route.tsx` does not register `/`. `group()` is assembled in `main.ts`,
not exported from a route or feature file.

## Table

`main.ts` imports the modules and calls `serve()`, `group()`, and `route()`.
That is the only place that binds a handler to a path.

## Fixtures

The main fixture (`int-tests/fixtures/app/`) matches this layout. Extra
fixtures that cannot share that `serve()` table are a small `main.ts` or
`main.tsx` in their own folder. They are not workspace members.

## Imports

App code imports from `dashi`. `dashi/jsx-runtime` is the Deno
`jsxImportSource` subpath, not a second public import style. The public vs
internal export list and the JSR `exports` map stay COO-27.
